<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Demo article for the read-aloud (embedded text-to-speech) feature.     -->
<!-- Exercises every content-policy case: prose, inline and display math,   -->
<!-- headings, lists, tables (announce-and-skip), code (announce-and-skip), -->
<!-- footnotes (silently skipped), hidden knowls, and abbreviations that    -->
<!-- stress the sentence segmenter.                                         -->
<pretext xml:lang="en-US">
  <docinfo>
    <latex-image-preamble>\usepackage{tikz}</latex-image-preamble>
  </docinfo>
  <article xml:id="read-aloud-demo">
    <title>Read-Aloud Demonstration</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This short article exists to be <em>listened to</em>.  Open the
        read-aloud player from the speaker button in the toolbar, press play,
        and follow along.  The reader speaks prose with your browser's voices
        and hands each equation to MathJax's speech text, so the mathematics
        is voiced in the same breath as the sentence around it.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-math-handoff">
      <title>The Mathematical Handoff</title>

      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x) = x^2</m>, which opens upward.  A
        generic screen-narrator would skip that expression or read glyph
        soup; here it is spoken inline.  Adjacent expressions like
        <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> should be separated cleanly, and an equation
        at the end of a sentence should keep its punctuation close, as with
        <m>e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Display mathematics becomes its own utterance, with a natural
        breath before and after:
        <me>\int_0^1 x^2 \, dx = \frac{1}{3}.</me>
        The decimal in <m>x \approx 3.2</m> must not split the sentence,
        and neither should the abbreviations in this one: see Dr. Smith,
        or e.g. Theorem 3.2, or cf. Fig. 4 for details.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-demo">
        <title>A Spoken Theorem</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>n</m> is an even integer, then <m>n^2</m> is divisible
            by <m>4</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Write <m>n = 2k</m> for some integer <m>k</m>.  Then
            <m>n^2 = 4k^2</m>, which is visibly a multiple of four.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-content-policy">
      <title>Announce and Skip</title>

      <p>
        The reader voices what a sighted reader would read aloud, and
        announces what it will not attempt.  A list is read item by item:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>first, plain prose in a list item;</p></li>
          <li><p>second, an item with math, namely <m>\sqrt{2}</m>;</p></li>
          <li><p>third, a short closing item.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        The table that follows should be announced and skipped, not read
        cell by cell.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-demo">
        <title>A table the reader skips</title>
        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Quantity</cell>
            <cell>Value</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Speed of sound</cell>
            <cell>343 m/s</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Likewise, the code listing below is announced and skipped.
      </p>

      <program language="python">
        <code>
def square(x):
    return x * x
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Images have their alt text read aloud.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="ex-fig">
        <caption>A sample figure containing an image.</caption>
        <image width="25%">
          <shortdescription>A drawing of a circle.</shortdescription>
          <description><p>
            A circle with radius <m>1</m> and therefore circumference <m>2\pi</m>.
          </p></description>
          <latex-image label="circle-img">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
              \draw (0,0) circle (1);
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-knowls">
      <title>Hidden Content and Navigation</title>

      <p>
        Everything below is born hidden, so a sighted reader sees only the
        titles until they click.  The <q>hidden content</q> setting decides
        what the listener gets: the titles alone, the whole thing, or a pause
        at each one.  Down-arrow dives into the most recent hidden block and
        up-arrow climbs back out, so the choice can be made block by block
        without changing the setting.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-even">
        <title>Even Integers</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            An integer <m>n</m> is <term>even</term> when <m>n = 2k</m> for
            some integer <m>k</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        A paragraph between two hidden blocks, so you can hear where one ends
        and the next begins.<fn>A footnote is hidden content too, and is announced and
        entered exactly like a knowl.</fn>
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-nested">
        <title>Squares of Even Integers</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>n</m> is even, then <m>n^2</m> is divisible by <m>4</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            This proof is hidden inside a hidden theorem, which is the case
            worth listening to: skipping the theorem should not announce this
            proof at all, and entering the theorem should announce it without
            reading it until you ask.
          </p>
          <p>
            Write <m>n = 2k</m>, so <m>n^2 = 4k^2</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <example xml:id="ex-aside">
        <title>A Hidden Example</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Take <m>n = 6</m>.  Then <m>n^2 = 36 = 4 \cdot 9</m>, as the
            theorem promises.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </example>

      <p>
        The last paragraph of the section.  Reaching it means every hidden
        block above was either read or stepped over.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-ending">
      <title>The End of the Page</title>

      <p>
        When the reader finishes the last block it announces the end of the
        page and offers to continue to the next section.  This is the final
        paragraph, so you should hear that announcement shortly.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</pretext>
